Chapter: TextEqBranches

 Equality on Text must answer the same thing whatever the branches around it
 are made of.

 The LIR selector fuses a comparison in condition position into a machine
 compare-and-branch. `==` on Text is a call to `__str_eq`, not a compare of
 the two values, so fusing it compared the two POINTERS: two equal strings at
 different addresses took the else arm. The tree emitter always guarded this
 in `is-fusable-int-comparison`; `lir-cmp-whitelisted` did not, so the bug
 appeared only where the LIR selector took the function.

 That is why the shape matters here rather than the comparison. `eq-int` and
 `eq-txt` compare the same two values with the same operator and differ only
 in what the arms return, and before the fix `eq-int` answered 0 while
 `eq-txt` answered "equal". Integer-typed arms made the function LIR-eligible;
 Text-typed arms did not.

 Every case below is built at runtime by `mk` rather than written as a
 literal, so a constant fold cannot answer for the emitter. The negative
 controls are what keep this honest: an implementation that always returns
 the else arm passes the equal cases only if the unequal ones fail with it.

 We say:

Section: Builders

  mk : Integer -> Text
  mk (n) = "x" & show n

Section: The shape that regressed -- Integer arms

  eq-int : Text, Text -> Integer
  eq-int (n) (p) = if n == p then 1 else 0

  ne-int : Text, Text -> Integer
  ne-int (n) (p) = if n /= p then 1 else 0

Section: The same comparison with other arm types

  eq-txt : Text, Text -> Text
  eq-txt (n) (p) = if n == p then "equal" else "differs"

  eq-bool : Text, Text -> Boolean
  eq-bool (n) (p) = n == p

Section: Integer operands in the identical shape, as the control

  eq-int-int : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  eq-int-int (a) (b) = if a == b then 1 else 0

Section: Cases

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  c1 : Text
  c1 = "eq-int same    " & show (eq-int (mk 5) (mk 5))

  c2 : Text
  c2 = "eq-int differ  " & show (eq-int (mk 5) (mk 6))

  c3 : Text
  c3 = "ne-int same    " & show (ne-int (mk 5) (mk 5))

  c4 : Text
  c4 = "ne-int differ  " & show (ne-int (mk 5) (mk 6))

  c5 : Text
  c5 = "eq-txt same    " & eq-txt (mk 5) (mk 5)

  c6 : Text
  c6 = "eq-txt differ  " & eq-txt (mk 5) (mk 6)

  c7 : Text
  c7 = "eq-bool same   " & yn (eq-bool (mk 5) (mk 5))

  c8 : Text
  c8 = "eq-bool differ " & yn (eq-bool (mk 5) (mk 6))

  c9 : Text
  c9 = "control int    " & show (eq-int-int 5 5) & show (eq-int-int 5 6)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni c1
    print-line-uni c2
    print-line-uni c3
    print-line-uni c4
    print-line-uni c5
    print-line-uni c6
    print-line-uni c7
    print-line-uni c8
    print-line-uni c9
  end
